n        return best\n","approach":"**Carry both the largest and the smallest product ending at each index, because a negative number swaps their roles.** Let `hi[i]` / `lo[i]` be the maximum / minimum product of a subarray ending at `i`. The subarray either starts at i (product `x`) or extends one ending at i-1, so\n\n`hi[i] = max(x, hi[i-1]*x, lo[i-1]*x)` and `lo[i] = min(x, hi[i-1]*x, lo[i-1]*x)`. The answer is `max(hi)`. Only the previous pair is needed, so two variables suffice.\n\n**Why it works:** the set of products of subarrays ending at i is `{x} \u222a {p*x : p ends at i-1}`; multiplying by a constant maps the extreme values of a set to extreme values, so tracking just the min and max of the previous set is sufficient. Zeros are handled because `x` itself (0) becomes both extremes and the next element restarts.\n\n**Common mistakes:** plain Kadane with products (fails on [-2,3,-4]), initialising best to 0 or 1 (fails on all-negative single element), and evaluating `cur_max` before computing `cur_min` from the old value.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** The two-pass trick: the answer is the max over prefix products and suffix products with resets at zero; explain why. Return the subarray bounds? (Store the start index alongside hi and lo.) What about floating point or very large products? (Use logarithms or track sign and count of negatives; a 32-bit overflow-safe variant.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["maximum-subarray","maximum-product-of-three-numbers","subarray-product-less-than-k"],"number":128,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}